ut and  and processing your your your sample  so our kind of solution that we came up  with  in our lab is this this concept of lab  on a particle  and the idea is quite simple and  and seems straightforward but took quite  a lot of time to develop  and the idea is that you could take  these if you take 3d structured  particles that have  unique shape and unique material  properties  can you spontaneously create uniform  compartments  in order to isolate say in this case for  example  cells of interest and any of these these  compartments or droplets formed by these  microparticles we refer to them as  tropicals  and we've been developing this system  over the last  four years now and we've uh here's just  kind of a  blanket view view summary of these  technologies we actually just released  quite a few pre-prints  in the last couple months on various  approaches that we have used  to create these these tropicals  i won't go into all these in detail i  just wanted to kind of  share that there is a multiple ways to  to  to create these lab on a particle  systems today i'll be focusing mostly on  on this work that i  i spearheaded which is using these  particles for doing  a single cell secretion assays  so i i kind of alluded to that we need  so we have these particles we need them  to be highly uniform  and we need them to have very distinct  shapes in this case i argue we need to  have some cavity to  maintain a cell inside of so how can we  create these and how can we create these  uniformly and high throughput  so although i argue that we don't want  to have to use  special equipment or say microfluidics  in the  end assay perhaps we can utilize it  up front in order to fabricate materials  that can then be distributed to other  people  and so that's exactly what we did so we  used a microfluidic droplet generator  in this case we're injecting a uv  cross-linkable  peg polymer phase with this it's it's  nice that it's very modifiable you can  add  different reactive groups such as a  biotin or  say a peptide sequence such as rgd that  allows for cell adhesion  in the other phase we have a dextran  polymer phase  so we mix these together we generate  droplets with these  and if you wait just a few seconds they  actually  they'll undergo phase separation so this  is these polymers are unique in that  they're an aqueous two-phase system  and if you tune the polymer  concentration just right you can get  this very distinct morphology  where you have a outer peg spherical  drop  and then a smaller spherical dextran  component maintained within that that  larger droplet  that's just barely touching the surface  now if you imagine you have these  droplets with these morphology  if you excite them with uv light to  cross-link the peg phase  and then wash to remove the oil and  transfer them back into water  you're left with these these sort of  bowl  or kind of suspendable microparticle  shaped  or microwell shaped particles that have  an exposed cavity  and the nice thing is you can create a  lot of these particles  and high throughput and then store them  for later use  and the point i want to kind of  emphasize here is that  in terms of doing the assays that i will  talk about further in this presentation  there will be no more microfluidics  so we are using the microfluidics up  front in order to fabricate these  smart biomaterials that we can then use  as part of a assay that is compatible  with just  simple common laboratory equipment  so great how can we actually  kind of meet the requirements that i set  forth earlier so the first thing is we  need to be able to create uniform  compartments  and uh the way we figured out to do this  quite  quite simply actually is if you if you  take a suspension of these particles  and you take some oil and surfactant and  if you just agitate it by pipetting  you can imagine that you will create  small smaller and smaller  water and oil droplets until eventually  you get  to the size of th